-- Q11202: Business Law: Contracts [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 265, frustration of purpose applies when a supervening event destroys the principal purpose without making performance impossible—Lawton can still pay rent but the fireworks ban eliminates the lease''s value. Under Section 261, impossibility applies to Garner because the hurricane destroyed the cabin, making renovation literally impossible.
Wrong (A): The contracts are not both discharged under impossibility. Lawton can still perform the lease; only the purpose is frustrated.
Wrong (B): Neither party assumed the risk of these specific supervening events, and both doctrines allow discharge in appropriate circumstances.
Wrong (D): This reverses the doctrines. Lawton''s situation involves frustration of purpose (performance possible but valueless), not impossibility.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11202;

-- Q11203: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 25, an option contract is a separate agreement in which the offeree pays consideration to keep the offer open for a specified period. During that time the offeror cannot revoke, even upon receiving a higher offer.
Wrong (B): An option contract does not allow the offeree to alter the offer''s terms; it merely preserves the existing offer as stated.
Wrong (C): An option contract does not transfer the offeror''s rights to a third party; it only binds the offeror to keep the offer open.
Wrong (D): An option contract creates no immediate obligation to perform—it only secures the offeree''s right to accept within the stated period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11203;

-- Q11204: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 281, an accord and satisfaction discharges a disputed obligation when the debtor tenders a lesser amount clearly marked as full payment and the creditor accepts. Because the debt was genuinely disputed and Crawford cashed the check marked ''payment in full,'' the entire obligation is discharged.
Wrong (A): Cashing a check conspicuously marked ''payment in full'' on a disputed claim constitutes acceptance of the accord, waiving the right to pursue the balance.
Wrong (C): The rule against partial payment discharging a larger debt applies only to undisputed (liquidated) debts; a bona fide dispute provides consideration for the accord.
Wrong (D): Accord and satisfaction does not require proof that the defects reduced value by the exact amount withheld—only a genuine dispute is required.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11204;

-- Q11205: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under UCC Section 2-608, a buyer may revoke acceptance when a nonconformity substantially impairs the goods'' value and the buyer accepted on the reasonable assumption the seller would cure. Norton relied on Quincy''s assurance, and six weeks of failed repairs show the cure did not occur.
Wrong (A): Acceptance is not always final under the UCC. Section 2-608 expressly permits revocation when substantial impairment exists and statutory conditions are met.
Wrong (B): Norton was not required to reject at delivery because acceptance based on a reasonable cure expectation is a recognized ground for later revocation.
Wrong (C): Section 2-608 does not impose a fixed 30-day deadline; revocation must occur within a reasonable time after the buyer discovers the nonconformity persists.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11205;

-- Q11206: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Third) of Restitution and Unjust Enrichment Section 1, a quasi-contract is a court-imposed obligation to prevent unjust enrichment when no actual contract exists. Nguyen received a measurable benefit from the plumbing repair and would be unjustly enriched without compensating Dawson.
Wrong (B): An implied-in-fact contract requires mutual assent inferred from the parties'' conduct. Here, Nguyen never assented to the repair, so no implied-in-fact contract exists.
Wrong (C): A voidable contract is one that exists but may be rescinded by a party. No contract of any kind was formed between Dawson and Nguyen.
Wrong (D): A quasi-contract does not require court approval to become binding—it is imposed by operation of law to remedy unjust enrichment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11206;

-- Q11207: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 79, courts generally do not inquire into the adequacy of consideration. As long as there is a bargained-for exchange of legal value, the contract is enforceable regardless of whether the values are proportionate.
Wrong (A): Courts do not require consideration to be equal in value. The law requires only that each side provide something of legal value, not equivalent value.
Wrong (B): Fair market value is not the standard for consideration. A bargained-for exchange at any price satisfies the consideration requirement.
Wrong (D): No independent appraisal is needed. Courts assess whether consideration exists, not whether it is adequate or proportional.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11207;

-- Q11208: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under UCC Section 2-509(3), a merchant seller retains risk of loss until the buyer takes physical possession, while a non-merchant seller''s risk passes upon tender of delivery. As a furniture store merchant, Palmer bears the loss; as a private seller, risk would have passed when the table was made available for pickup.
Wrong (A): Payment alone does not determine risk of loss under the UCC. The statute distinguishes between merchant and non-merchant sellers, not between paid and unpaid transactions.
Wrong (C): This reverses the rule. For merchants, risk does not pass upon payment; it passes upon physical possession. For non-merchants, risk passes upon tender, not physical possession.
Wrong (D): Risk is not always on the seller. For a non-merchant, risk passes upon tender of delivery even if the buyer has not yet picked up the goods.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11208;

-- Q11209: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 224, a condition precedent must occur before a party''s duty to perform is triggered. Because Delaney''s mortgage was denied, the condition failed and the duty to close never arose, regardless of whether Delaney notified Bennett.
Wrong (A): Failure to notify Bennett does not convert the failed condition into an obligation to perform. The condition precedent failed on its own terms when the mortgage was denied.
Wrong (B): A mortgage contingency is a true condition precedent that excuses performance, not merely a timing provision that delays it.
Wrong (C): The condition''s failure excuses performance without requiring Delaney to prove the denial was not self-caused, unless the contract imposed a good-faith effort obligation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11209;

-- Q11210: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under UCC Section 2-313, an express warranty arises from any affirmation of fact or promise that becomes part of the basis of the bargain. The mileage claim (35 mpg) is a specific, verifiable factual assertion that creates an express warranty.
Wrong (B): Not every seller statement creates a warranty. General opinions or commendations (puffery) are excluded under Section 2-313(2).
Wrong (C): The word ''warranty'' is not required. Any specific factual assertion about the goods can create an express warranty regardless of the terminology used.
Wrong (D): ''Finest sedan on the market'' is puffery—a general opinion no reasonable buyer would rely on as a factual representation—and does not create a warranty.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11210;

-- Q11211: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under UCC Section 2-715(1), incidental damages are the reasonable expenses directly resulting from the seller''s breach, including costs of inspection, transportation, storage, and arranging substitute performance. These compensate for immediate out-of-pocket costs of dealing with the breach.
Wrong (A): Pre-agreed damages specified in the contract are liquidated damages, not incidental damages. Liquidated damages are governed by UCC Section 2-718.
Wrong (C): Damages designed to punish the breaching party are punitive damages, which are generally not available in contract actions under the UCC.
Wrong (D): The contract-market price differential describes the standard measure of expectation damages under UCC Section 2-713, not incidental damages.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11211;

-- Q11212: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under UCC Section 2-306(1), an output contract is enforceable even without a stated quantity, but the quantity tendered must not be unreasonably disproportionate to any stated estimate or prior normal output. A 40% increase above historical average may be challenged as unreasonably disproportionate.
Wrong (A): The seller cannot demand acceptance of any quantity. The good-faith limitation in Section 2-306 prevents tendering unreasonably disproportionate amounts.
Wrong (B): Output contracts are not illusory. The good-faith obligation to produce and sell actual output supplies the consideration that prevents illusoriness.
Wrong (D): The absence of a fixed quantity does not render the contract unenforceable. UCC Section 2-306 expressly validates output and requirements contracts.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11212;

-- Q11213: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 178, a contract formed for an illegal purpose is void ab initio—treated as though it never existed. Courts will leave both parties where they stand and refuse enforcement.
Wrong (A): A voidable contract remains valid until one party elects to rescind. An illegal contract is void, not voidable, and cannot be ratified.
Wrong (B): Mutual consent to an illegal purpose does not make the contract enforceable. Illegality renders the agreement void regardless of the parties'' intent.
Wrong (C): An illegal contract is not merely ''valid but subject to penalties.'' The contract itself has no legal effect and cannot be enforced by either party.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11213;

-- Q11214: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 373, a non-breaching party is entitled to restitution of any benefit conferred on the breaching party to prevent unjust enrichment. Walters recovers the full $15,000 deposit because Henderson breached by refusing to complete the boat.
Wrong (B): Henderson''s $8,000 expenditure on materials is Henderson''s own cost of performance and does not reduce the restitution owed to Walters.
Wrong (C): $7,000 incorrectly offsets Henderson''s expenses against the deposit. The breaching party''s costs are not deducted from the non-breaching party''s restitution claim.
Wrong (D): $23,000 improperly adds Henderson''s material costs to Walters''s deposit. Restitution restores only the benefit Walters conferred, not Henderson''s own expenditures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11214;

-- Q11215: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 177, undue influence occurs when a party in a position of trust or authority exploits that relationship to pressure the weaker party into an unfavorable agreement. Martin''s role as live-in caretaker managing Eleanor''s finances created a confidential relationship susceptible to such abuse.
Wrong (A): A material misrepresentation of fact with intent to deceive describes fraud, not undue influence. Undue influence involves relationship exploitation, not factual deception.
Wrong (C): Physical threats or coercion describe duress, not undue influence. Undue influence involves subtle exploitation of a fiduciary relationship without overt threats.
Wrong (D): A genuine factual error shared by both parties describes mutual mistake, which is a distinct defense unrelated to relationship abuse.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11215;

-- Q11217: Business Law: Contracts [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 261, a contract is discharged by impossibility when the specific subject matter essential to performance is destroyed through no fault of either party. The earthquake destroyed the building wall, making the mural contract impossible to perform as agreed.
Wrong (A): A force majeure clause is not required for the impossibility doctrine to apply. Impossibility based on destruction of subject matter is a common law defense available regardless of contract terms.
Wrong (B): Courts do not order specific performance on a substitute subject matter. The contract specified the particular wall, and substitution would alter the agreement''s essential terms.
Wrong (C): Personal services contracts can be discharged by impossibility when the subject matter is destroyed. The rule barring impossibility applies to difficulty or increased cost, not physical destruction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11217;

-- Q11219: Business Law: Contracts [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under UCC Section 2-706, when a seller resells goods after buyer repudiation, the seller recovers the contract-resale differential plus incidental damages. Here: $200,000 − $120,000 = $80,000 shortfall, plus $4,000 storage and $1,500 brokerage = $85,500 total.
Wrong (A): Cover damages under Section 2-712 are a buyer''s remedy for obtaining substitute goods, not a seller''s remedy after repudiation.
Wrong (C): The full contract price under Section 2-709 is available only when goods cannot be resold at a reasonable price. Atlas successfully resold, making this remedy unavailable.
Wrong (D): Section 2-708 provides the contract-market differential, but it does not exclude incidental damages. Moreover, the resale remedy under Section 2-706 is the appropriate measure here.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11219;

-- Q11220: Business Structures [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under RUPA Section 601, a partner may dissociate at any time by express will. Section 701 requires the partnership to buy out the dissociated partner''s interest at fair value. Unlike the older UPA, RUPA distinguishes dissociation from dissolution, so the partnership may continue.
Wrong (B): Dissociation does not automatically dissolve the partnership or impose a 90-day wind-up period. Under RUPA, the remaining partner may elect to continue the business.
Wrong (C): Under RUPA Section 703, a dissociated partner''s liability for future obligations is limited to two years after dissociation, not indefinite until formal state filing.
Wrong (D): A partner has the power to dissociate unilaterally under RUPA Section 601(1). The dissociation is effective even without the other partners'' consent.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11220;

-- Q11221: Business Structures [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 11(b), the corporation pays 21% tax on $200,000 = $42,000. The remaining $158,000 distributed as qualified dividends is taxed at 15% under IRC Section 1(h) = $23,700. Combined burden: $42,000 + $23,700 = $65,700, illustrating C corporation double taxation.
Wrong (A): $42,000 reflects only the corporate-level tax and ignores the 15% shareholder-level dividend tax on the $158,000 distribution.
Wrong (C): $72,000 would result from applying a combined 36% rate to the full $200,000, which incorrectly treats corporate and shareholder taxes as additive percentages on the same base.
Wrong (D): $30,000 reflects only the shareholder-level dividend tax applied to $200,000 at 15%, ignoring both the corporate tax and the correct distribution base.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11221;

-- Q11222: Business Structures [medium/L2]
