-- Q11231: Business Structures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under 11 U.S.C. Section 303(b), when a debtor has 12 or more creditors, an involuntary petition must be filed by three or more creditors whose aggregate unsecured noncontingent claims total at least the statutory minimum ($18,600). Dalton has 15 creditors, so the three-creditor rule applies.
Wrong (A): A single creditor may file only when the debtor has fewer than 12 creditors. With 15 creditors, at least three petitioning creditors are required.
Wrong (C): The statute does not require a majority of creditors by dollar amount. It requires a minimum number of petitioning creditors meeting a dollar threshold.
Wrong (D): A single creditor cannot file regardless of claim amount when the debtor has 12 or more creditors. The three-creditor requirement is mandatory.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11231;

-- Q11232: Business Structures [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under 11 U.S.C. Section 707(b), when a debtor''s current monthly income exceeds the state median for the applicable household size, a presumption of abuse arises under the means test. The case may be dismissed or converted to Chapter 13 to require a repayment plan.
Wrong (A): Chapter 7 is not automatically available to all individuals. The means test specifically limits access for above-median-income debtors to prevent abuse.
Wrong (B): The means test is based on income relative to the state median, not on whether the debtor''s assets exceed exemption limits. Asset levels are a separate consideration.
Wrong (D): There is no statutory requirement to repay exactly 50% of debts. If the case is converted to Chapter 13, the repayment plan is based on disposable income, not a fixed percentage.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11232;
